---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import ReadingSpeedTest from "../../components/ReadingSpeedTest.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import { resolveCanonicalUrl } from "../../config/site";

const pageTitle =
	"Reading Speed Test & Online WPM Reading Tester | Wordora";
const pageDescription =
	"Take a free 1-minute reading speed test and words per minute reading test. Use our online reading speed tester to test your reading speed (WPM) and compare by grade level.";
const canonicalUrl = resolveCanonicalUrl("/reading-speed-test/");

const webAppSchema = {
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "WebApplication",
	name: "Reading Speed Test & WPM Comprehension Quiz",
	url: canonicalUrl,
	applicationCategory: "EducationalApplication",
	operatingSystem: "Any",
	offers: {
		"@type": "Offer",
		price: "0",
		priceCurrency: "USD",
	},
	description: pageDescription,
	featureList: [
		"Calibrated non-fiction passages in Short (~150 words), Medium (~300 words), and Long (~500 words) lengths",
		"Live stopwatch timer with Start Reading and Finish Reading controls",
		"Instant Words Per Minute (WPM) calculation and comparison against the 238 WPM adult silent reading average",
		"3-question and 5-question reading comprehension check with Effective WPM score",
		"RSVP speed trainer mode for progressive pacing drills from 150 to 800 WPM",
		"100% private client-side execution in your browser",
	],
};

const faqList = [
	{
		question: "How do I test my reading speed accurately?",
		answer:
			"To measure your true reading speed, time yourself reading an unfamiliar passage silently at your natural pace without pausing or re-reading lines, then answer comprehension questions from memory. Divide the total word count by your reading time in seconds and multiply by 60 to get your raw WPM, then multiply that score by your quiz accuracy percentage to find your Effective WPM.",
	},
	{
		question: "What is a good reading speed score for an adult?",
		answer:
			"For most adults reading standard non-fiction, a good reading speed is between 238 and 300 WPM with at least 75% to 80% comprehension. Scores above 320 WPM with strong recall put you ahead of roughly 80% of adult readers, while scores above 400 WPM are considered fast.",
	},
	{
		question: "How many words per minute should a 6th grader read?",
		answer:
			"A typical 6th grader (ages 11–12) reads roughly 150 to 205 words per minute (WPM) during silent reading with at least 75% comprehension, and about 130 to 160 WPM when reading aloud for oral fluency benchmarks.",
	},
	{
		question: "Is 200 WPM a slow reading speed?",
		answer:
			"No, 200 WPM is not unusually slow—it sits right at the lower end of the normal adult range (200 to 300 WPM). If you are reading dense technical textbooks, academic papers, or material in a second language, 200 WPM is a healthy, careful pace that supports deep understanding.",
	},
	{
		question: "Is reading 400 WPM fast?",
		answer:
			"Yes, 400 WPM is nearly 70% faster than the adult average of 238 WPM. At 400 WPM, you can read roughly 24,000 words per hour—or about 80 standard paperback pages per hour—making it a strong, efficient pace for university students, researchers, and avid readers.",
	},
	{
		question: "Is 500 WPM considered fast reading?",
		answer:
			"Yes, 500 WPM is very fast and places you in roughly the top 1% to 2% of natural adult readers, provided your comprehension stays above 75%. At 500 WPM, you read 30,000 words—or roughly 100 paperback pages—every hour.",
	},
	{
		question: "Are 800, 900, or 1,000 WPM reading speeds humanly possible?",
		answer:
			"Eye-tracking and cognitive psychology research shows that 700 to 900 WPM is the upper biological ceiling for genuine reading with full comprehension. Speeds of 1,000 WPM or higher are classified as skimming—where your eyes sample keywords and skip filler words rather than reading every sentence.",
	},
	{
		question: "What is the top 1% of reading speeds?",
		answer:
			"Among adults who maintain high comprehension (80% or higher on recall tests), the top 1% of readers typically read between 500 and 700 WPM. Claims of 1,500+ WPM almost always reflect rapid skimming rather than full-text comprehension.",
	},
	{
		question: "How fast is 100 pages an hour, and is 20 pages an hour slow?",
		answer:
			"Reading 100 standard paperback pages (~300 words per page) in an hour equals 500 WPM, which is exceptionally fast. Conversely, 20 pages an hour equals roughly 100 WPM—slow for casual fiction, though completely normal when studying complex STEM textbooks, legal documents, or philosophy.",
	},
	{
		question: "Can you read 3,000 words in 15 minutes?",
		answer:
			"Yes—reading 3,000 words in 15 minutes requires an average speed of 200 WPM (3,000 divided by 15 equals 200). Because 200 WPM is right at the baseline adult reading speed, almost any adult reader can finish a 3,000-word article in 15 minutes comfortably.",
	},
	{
		question: "Is reading 200 pages a day a lot?",
		answer:
			"Yes, 200 pages a day is a heavy daily reading volume. At 300 words per page (60,000 words total), finishing 200 pages takes an average adult reader (238 WPM) roughly 4 hours and 12 minutes of focused reading per day, or about 2 hours for a fast 500 WPM reader.",
	},
	{
		question: "What is the 50-page book rule?",
		answer:
			"The 50-page book rule (popularized by librarian Nancy Pearl) is a practical reading habit: give any new book 50 pages to capture your interest, and if it still feels like a chore by page 50, put it down guilt-free and move on to a better book. For readers over age 50, the rule playfully adjusts to subtracting your age from 100 (100 minus age equals pages to try).",
	},
	{
		question: "What is the 5-finger rule for books?",
		answer:
			"The 5-finger rule is a quick way for kids, parents, and students to check if a book matches their reading level. Open to any full page and raise one finger for each unfamiliar or difficult word: 0–1 fingers means the book is very easy, 2–3 fingers is the ideal \"just right\" challenge, and 4–5 fingers means the vocabulary may be too frustrating without help.",
	},
	{
		question: "Is reading speed related to IQ?",
		answer:
			"No—reading speed has only a weak correlation with general IQ. How fast you read is driven much more by instant word recognition, vocabulary size, and daily reading practice than raw intelligence, and many brilliant thinkers intentionally read slowly to analyze complex ideas.",
	},
	{
		question: "Do people with ADHD read faster or slower?",
		answer:
			"People with ADHD can experience both depending on the text. Routine or uninteresting passages often result in slower speeds (140 to 190 WPM) due to attentional drift and re-reading lines, while high-interest topics can trigger hyperfocus, pushing reading speeds above 300 WPM.",
	},
];

const faqPageSchema = {
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "FAQPage",
	mainEntity: faqList.map((item) => ({
		"@type": "Question",
		name: item.question,
		acceptedAnswer: {
			"@type": "Answer",
			text: item.answer,
		},
	})),
};
---

<Layout
	title={pageTitle}
	description={pageDescription}
	canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
>
	<Fragment slot="head">
		<script
			type="application/ld+json"
			set:html={JSON.stringify(webAppSchema)}
		/>
		<script
			type="application/ld+json"
			set:html={JSON.stringify(faqPageSchema)}
		/>
	</Fragment>

	<Header />

	<main class="flex-1">
		<!-- Clean Editorial Tool Header -->
		<section class="px-4 pt-12 pb-4 sm:px-6 sm:pt-16 sm:pb-6">
			<div class="mx-auto max-w-[880px] text-center">
				<h1
					class="font-sans text-[32px] leading-[38px] font-semibold tracking-[-1.4px] text-ink sm:text-[42px] sm:leading-[48px] sm:tracking-[-1.8px]"
				>
					Reading Speed Test
				</h1>

				<p
					class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[16px] leading-[25px] font-normal text-body"
				>
					Measure your natural silent reading rate in Words Per Minute (WPM), compare your pace against the 238 WPM adult average, and check your comprehension with 3 quick questions.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<!-- Interactive Reading Speed Test Workspace -->
		<ReadingSpeedTest />

		<section class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-10 sm:px-6 md:mt-12 md:pt-12">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How to Use This Reading Speed Tester &amp; Words Per Minute Reading Test
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Follow these four quick steps in our online <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">reading speed tester</strong> to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">test your reading speed</strong> and get an accurate baseline of both your raw pace and your real-world comprehension score:
			</p>

			<ol class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[#0f2e26] text-[12px] font-semibold text-[#afe3d0] tabular-nums dark:bg-[#afe3d0] dark:text-[#0f2e26]">1</span>
						<h3 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Configure Your Test (Setup)</h3>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Choose <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Standard Reading Test</strong> to measure your natural silent pace, or <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">RSVP Speed Trainer</strong> to practice paced flashing at 150–800 WPM. Pick a difficulty level (<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Beginner</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Intermediate</strong>, or <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Challenge</strong>) or <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Paste Your Text</strong>, set your quiz length and font size, and click <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Start Reading Test</strong>.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[#0f2e26] text-[12px] font-semibold text-[#afe3d0] tabular-nums dark:bg-[#afe3d0] dark:text-[#0f2e26]">2</span>
						<h3 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Read Silently at Your Natural Pace (Read)</h3>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Read the passage smoothly from start to finish without rushing or skimming. As soon as your eyes reach the final word, click <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Finish Reading</strong> so the timer records your exact elapsed seconds.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[#0f2e26] text-[12px] font-semibold text-[#afe3d0] tabular-nums dark:bg-[#afe3d0] dark:text-[#0f2e26]">3</span>
						<h3 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Answer the Recall Questions (Quiz)</h3>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						The passage hides automatically after you finish reading so you cannot scan back for answers. Complete the quick comprehension questions from memory to verify how much information you actually retained.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated p-5">
					<div class="mb-2 flex items-center gap-2.5">
						<span class="flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-[#0f2e26] text-[12px] font-semibold text-[#afe3d0] tabular-nums dark:bg-[#afe3d0] dark:text-[#0f2e26]">4</span>
						<h3 class="text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">Review Your Raw &amp; Effective WPM (Results)</h3>
					</div>
					<p class="text-[14.5px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						See your <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Raw WPM</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Comprehension Accuracy</strong>, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Effective WPM</strong> compared against the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">238 WPM adult average</strong>, and track your improvement over time inside <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Past Results</strong>.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How to Test Reading Speed: Reading WPM Test (Raw WPM vs. Effective WPM)
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Most people who guess how fast they read end up off by a wide margin. Without a stopwatch and a known word count, it is almost impossible to tell whether you are moving steadily through a page or quietly losing minutes to re-reading the same lines. Taking a calibrated <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">reading wpm test</strong> and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">words per minute reading test</strong> removes the guesswork by timing a clean, uninterrupted passage and converting your elapsed seconds into <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Words Per Minute (WPM)</strong> using a simple ratio:
			</p>

			<div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated px-5 py-4 text-center">
				<p class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink sm:text-[16px]">
					WPM = (Total Words Read &divide; Elapsed Seconds) &times; 60
				</p>
			</div>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				If you finish a 300-word passage in 75 seconds, your raw reading speed is 240 WPM. That number gives you an honest starting point—provided the test prevents two common mistakes. First, you should not be able to read ahead before the timer starts, which is why the test above keeps the passage hidden until you click <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Start Reading Test</strong>. Second, once you finish reading, the text disappears before you answer comprehension questions so your score reflects genuine recall rather than scanning back for answers.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Why Raw WPM Alone Only Tells Half the Story
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Anyone can sweep their eyes across 500 words in fifteen seconds, log an impressive 2,000 WPM, and walk away unable to summarize a single sentence. In cognitive science and reading research, speed without understanding is classified as skimming—not reading.
			</p>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				To measure real-world reading ability, educators and researchers rely on <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Effective WPM</strong>, which adjusts your raw pace by how much you actually understood and remembered:
			</p>

			<div class="rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated px-5 py-4 text-center">
				<p class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-ink sm:text-[16px]">
					Effective WPM = Raw WPM &times; Comprehension Accuracy
				</p>
			</div>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Consider two readers tackling the same 300-word article:
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Reader A</strong> moves at a calm, focused <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">290 WPM</strong> and answers 4 out of 5 comprehension questions correctly (<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">80% accuracy</strong>). Their Effective WPM is <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">232 WPM</strong>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Reader B</strong> races through the text at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">420 WPM</strong> but misses three questions (<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">40% accuracy</strong>). Their Effective WPM drops to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">168 WPM</strong>.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Even though Reader B finished faster on the clock, Reader A absorbed substantially more usable information per minute. Whenever you test yourself, treat Effective WPM—not raw speed—as the score you want to improve over time.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Choosing the Right Mode for Your Goal
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Different reading tasks call for different ways of testing and practicing, which is why the tool above includes three distinct options:
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Standard Reading Test (Beginner, Intermediate, and Challenge):</strong> Choose this when you want an accurate baseline of your natural silent reading speed. Short passages (~150 words) work well for a quick check, while longer passages (~500 words) reveal whether your focus and stamina hold up over multiple paragraphs.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Paste Your Text:</strong> Your reading speed changes depending on what is in front of you. A familiar news story reads much faster than a dense university chapter or a legal contract. Paste an excerpt from whatever you are currently studying or editing to see your true pace on real material.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">RSVP Speed Trainer (One Word or Sentence at a Time):</strong> Rapid Serial Visual Presentation flashes words in a fixed spot so your eyes do not have to jump across lines. Rather than using RSVP to measure your everyday baseline, treat it as a training drill to practice steady forward pacing at a target WPM without habitual backtracking.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Once you know your personal WPM, you can plug that number into our <a href="/reading-time-calculator/" class="font-medium text-vercel-blue underline decoration-vercel-blue/30 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-vercel-blue-deep hover:decoration-vercel-blue">Reading Time Calculator</a> to see exactly how many minutes an article, study chapter, or full book will take you to finish.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Average Reading Speed by Age, Grade Level, and Reader Profile
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				When people ask, <em class="italic text-ink">"What is the average reading speed?"</em> they usually expect a single magic number. In reality, reading researchers—including decades of eye-tracking studies summarized by cognitive psychologist Marc Brysbaert—have shown that a single universal average is mostly a convenient fiction. Your reading speed shifts dramatically depending on your age, schooling, vocabulary familiarity, and whether you are reading silently or out loud.
			</p>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Across large-scale meta-analyses of English-language studies, the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">average adult silent reading speed for standard non-fiction is 238 words per minute (WPM)</strong>, while general fiction averages slightly higher at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">260 WPM</strong>. For most healthy adults, anything between <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">200 and 300 WPM</strong> with at least 75% comprehension is a solid, functional pace.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				How Reading Speed Develops From Childhood to Older Adulthood
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Reading fluency builds in predictable stages. Young children spend most of their mental effort decoding individual letters and syllables, which keeps early elementary speeds modest. As word recognition becomes automatic between 3rd and 8th grade, silent reading overtakes oral speech (~183 WPM) and climbs steadily through high school and university.
			</p>

			<div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated">
				<table class="w-full min-w-[600px] border-collapse text-left text-[14px]">
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline bg-canvas text-[13px] font-semibold text-ink">
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Age / Grade Level</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Typical Age</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Silent Reading Speed (WPM)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3">What Happens at This Stage</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline text-body">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">1st Grade</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">6–7 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">53 – 80 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Phonics decoding and sounding out individual words aloud</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">2nd Grade</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">7–8 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">89 – 110 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Recognizing common sight words and building sentence rhythm</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">3rd Grade</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">8–9 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">107 – 135 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Transitioning from learning to read to reading for meaning</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">4th &amp; 5th Grade (KS2)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">9–11 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">123 – 160 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Silent reading begins pulling ahead of spoken reading pace</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Middle School (6th–8th Grade)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">11–14 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">150 – 204 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Expanding academic vocabulary and multi-paragraph stamina</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">High School (9th–12th Grade)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">14–18 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">200 – 250 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Approaching adult baseline across textbooks and literature</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">College / University Student</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">18–22 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">240 – 300 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Peak daily reading volume and rapid synthesis of arguments</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Average Adult (US &amp; Global)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">23–59 years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">238 WPM (200–300)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Benchmark silent reading rate for everyday non-fiction prose</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Older Adults (60–64+ Years)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">60+ years</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">190 – 225 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Slightly slower visual processing, offset by deep vocabulary</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Fast / Avid Reader</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Adult</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">300 – 450 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Minimal backtracking and strong phrase-level chunking</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Top 1% of Readers</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Adult</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">500 – 700 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Upper physiological limit while maintaining high comprehension</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Why Older Adults Often Read Slightly Slower—Yet Understand More
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				If you are in your 60s or 70s and notice your score sitting around <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">195 to 215 WPM</strong> rather than 250 WPM, that slight dip is completely normal. Eye-tracking research shows that older readers make slightly longer eye pauses (<em>fixations</em>) and occasional backward checks to verify fine print or complex phrasing. However, because older adults possess substantially larger vocabularies and broader background knowledge than college students, their <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">comprehension and retention scores frequently match or beat younger readers</strong>.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Where Does the "Top 1%" Really Sit?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Online speed-reading ads often claim that top readers cruise at 1,500 or 2,000 WPM. Controlled laboratory tests tell a very different story. Even among PhD students, editors, and lifelong bookworms, sustained reading with strong comprehension rarely exceeds <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">500 to 700 WPM</strong>. If your score lands above <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">320 WPM</strong> with 80% or higher quiz accuracy, you are already reading faster than roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">80% of the adult population</strong>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
