			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Is 100 Pages an Hour Fast—and Is 20 Pages an Hour Slow?
			</h3>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">100 pages an hour</strong> in a standard 300-word-per-page book equals <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">500 WPM</strong> (or about <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">415 WPM</strong> in a looser 250-word-per-page novel). That places you in the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">top 1% to 2% of natural readers</strong>, provided you are actually absorbing the plot or arguments rather than skipping paragraphs.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">20 pages an hour</strong> translates to roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">100 WPM</strong>. For casual fiction, that is slower than the adult average—often caused by frequent backtracking or reading in a noisy environment. However, if you are working through dense organic chemistry, legal statutes, or philosophy, 20 pages an hour is completely normal because you are pausing to analyze formulas and logic.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Screen vs. Paper: Does Format Change Your Page Rate?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Controlled studies comparing printed books against backlit laptops and tablets consistently find that people read <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">5% to 10% slower on screens</strong> when tackling long, complex texts, and often experience slightly lower recall due to scrolling fatigue and visual glare. E-ink readers (like Kindle) perform nearly identically to physical paper. If you want to convert an exact word count into page totals for manuscripts or printed books, check our <a href="/words-to-pages/" class="font-medium text-vercel-blue underline decoration-vercel-blue/30 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-vercel-blue-deep hover:decoration-vercel-blue">Words to Pages Converter</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				The Cognitive Science of Reading: Saccades, Fixations, and Subvocalization
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				To understand why some people read effortlessly at 400 WPM while others stall at 170 WPM, you have to look at what your eyes and brain are actually doing on the page. Reading feels like a smooth, continuous glide from left to right—but in reality, your eyes move in a rapid series of tiny hops and stops.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				1. Saccades and Fixations: How Your Eyes Capture Words
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				During silent reading, your eyes perform quick, ballistic jumps called <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">saccades</strong> (each taking roughly 20 to 35 milliseconds), separated by brief pauses called <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">fixations</strong> (each lasting about 200 to 250 milliseconds). Your brain only absorbs visual information while the eye is stopped during a fixation—never while it is moving.
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Perceptual Span:</strong> During a single fixation in English, sharp central vision captures roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">7 to 9 letter spaces</strong> to the right of your gaze, while peripheral vision previews upcoming word shapes and spaces.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Why Fast Readers Move Differently:</strong> Fast readers do not move their eyeballs faster; rather, they recognize familiar word patterns more quickly, make slightly shorter fixations, and absorb 2 to 3 short words per stop instead of halting on every single word.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				2. Regressions: The Hidden Habit That Slows You Down
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">10% to 15% of all eye movements</strong> during normal reading are <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">regressions</strong>—quick backward jumps to re-read a word or phrase you already passed.
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Helpful vs. Habitual Regressions:</strong> When you hit a confusing sentence or an unexpected plot twist, a quick backward glance helps comprehension. However, many readers develop a nervous habit of constantly double-checking lines even when they understood them the first time, cutting their effective speed by 20% to 30%.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				3. Subvocalization: Should You Silence Your Inner Voice?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Subvocalization</strong> is the faint internal voice you "hear" in your head as you read, accompanied by microscopic muscle signals in your vocal cords. Commercial speed-reading courses often claim that eliminating subvocalization is the secret to reading 1,000+ WPM.
			</p>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Cognitive science tells a more nuanced truth:
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					Your inner voice is tied directly to phonological memory—it helps your brain hold complex clauses together and verify meaning.
				</li>
				<li>
					Trying to force zero inner voice on dense non-fiction usually destroys comprehension. Instead of trying to eliminate subvocalization completely, skilled readers simply <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">reduce word-by-word pronunciation</strong> by grouping familiar phrases together visually.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Is Speed Reading at 800 to 1,000+ WPM Really Possible?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Because human eye fixations and lexical recognition have hard biological speed limits, cognitive psychologists—including Keith Rayner and Elizabeth Schotter—have demonstrated that <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">true reading with full comprehension tops out around 700 to 900 WPM</strong>. Anyone claiming 1,500 or 2,000 WPM is <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">skimming</strong> (sampling keywords and guessing the rest), which can be useful for previewing a long report, but is no substitute for deep reading.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How to Increase Your Reading Speed Without Losing Comprehension
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Improving your reading speed does not require expensive courses or mystical eye exercises. Because reading is a cognitive skill built on pattern recognition, vocabulary, and attention, most adults and students can realistically increase their comfortable reading pace by <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">30% to 50% over four to six weeks</strong>—for example, moving from 220 WPM to 310 WPM—while keeping comprehension above 80%.
			</p>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Here are five evidence-backed techniques that genuinely work:
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				1. Use a Visual Pacer (The Pointer Method)
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Your eyes are naturally drawn to motion. When you guide your gaze smoothly under each line using your fingertip, a pen, or a cursor on screen, two things happen immediately: your eyes spend less time wandering, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">habitual regressions drop sharply</strong>. Set a steady, slightly brisk sweep across each line so your eyes keep moving forward rather than stalling on familiar words.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				2. Group Words Into Meaningful Phrases (Chunking)
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Instead of stopping on every individual word (<em>The</em> / <em>quick</em> / <em>brown</em> / <em>fox</em>), train yourself to absorb <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">2 to 4 words per eye fixation</strong> (<em>The quick brown fox</em> / <em>jumps over</em> / <em>the lazy dog</em>). Start by focusing slightly inside the left margin and stopping a word or two before the right margin—letting your peripheral vision capture the first and last words of each line automatically.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				3. Preview Structure Before Reading Deeply
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				For non-fiction books, textbooks, and long articles, spend <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">60 seconds scanning headings, bolded terms, tables, and the opening and closing paragraphs</strong> before you start reading word-for-word. When your brain already knows the roadmap of an author's argument, it processes unfamiliar sentences much faster and retains key points with far less effort.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				4. Practice Progressive RSVP Pacing Drills
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Switch the tool above to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">RSVP Speed Trainer</strong> and set the pace <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">30 to 50 WPM faster than your current baseline</strong> for a short 2-minute drill. Because words appear one at a time in a fixed spot, you experience what faster forward processing feels like without backward jumps. Afterward, return to normal page reading at a slightly slower, comfortable speed—your everyday pace will naturally feel easier and quicker.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				5. Build Domain Vocabulary and Read Daily
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				The single strongest predictor of reading speed is <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">instant word recognition</strong>. Whenever you read heavily in a specific field—whether software engineering, medicine, law, or history—your brain stores common terminology as single visual units. Reading for just <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">20 to 30 minutes every day</strong> on progressively richer material compounds both speed and comprehension far more reliably than occasional cramming.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Reading Speed, IQ, Neurodiversity (ADHD), and Famous Readers
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Whenever reading speed comes up in online discussions, three popular questions follow right behind: whether fast readers have higher IQs, how conditions like ADHD affect reading pace, and whether famous polymaths really devour books at superhuman speeds. Separating cognitive science from internet folklore helps you set realistic expectations for your own progress.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Is Reading Speed Related to IQ?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				No—reading speed is <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">not a direct measure of intelligence</strong>. Cognitive psychology studies consistently show only a weak correlation between general IQ and words per minute.
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">What Actually Drives Speed:</strong> Reading speed depends primarily on <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">visual decoding fluency</strong>, <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">print exposure (how much you read)</strong>, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">vocabulary familiarity</strong>, rather than raw reasoning power.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Why Deep Thinkers Often Read Slowly:</strong> Many high-IQ scientists, philosophers, and lawyers deliberately read at <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">150 to 220 WPM</strong> because they pause to question assumptions, connect ideas across chapters, and evaluate evidence. Reading slowly to think deeply is a strength, not a limitation.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Do People With ADHD Read Faster or Slower?
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD) affects reading pace in two opposite directions depending on interest and environment:
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Slower Pace on Routine Text:</strong> Because ADHD impacts working memory and sustained visual attention, readers often lose their place on the page, skip lines, or re-read the same paragraph multiple times—pulling everyday reading speed down toward <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">140 to 190 WPM</strong>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Hyperfocus on Engaging Topics:</strong> When an ADHD reader encounters a subject that genuinely sparks curiosity, intense <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">hyperfocus</strong> can lock in attention and push reading pace well above <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">300 WPM</strong>. Using a visual pacer (like a finger or cursor) or short timed blocks often helps bridge that gap on routine study material.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Famous Readers: Myth vs. Reality (JFK and Bill Gates)
			</h3>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">John F. Kennedy (The 1,200 WPM Myth):</strong> Newspaper stories from the 1960s famously claimed President Kennedy could read 1,200 WPM after taking a speed-reading course. Even his instructors later acknowledged that 1,200 WPM reflected <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">rapid skimming of morning newspapers and briefings</strong>, not word-for-word reading of complex books.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Bill Gates (~500–600 WPM With Margin Notes):</strong> Bill Gates reads roughly 50 non-fiction books a year at an estimated <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">500 to 600 WPM</strong>—fast, yet entirely within the upper physiological range of practiced adult readers. Notably, he refuses to start a book he will not finish and writes notes in the margins to ensure deep retention rather than chasing raw speed.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				How Content Type and Format Change Your Reading Speed
			</h2>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				A skilled reader never uses the exact same speed for every piece of text. Just as you shift gears when driving from a flat highway onto a winding mountain road, your brain automatically adjusts its reading pace depending on sentence complexity, unfamiliar vocabulary, and why you are reading in the first place.
			</p>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Reading Speed Across Different Text Types
			</h3>

			<div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated">
				<table class="w-full min-w-[600px] border-collapse text-left text-[14px]">
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline bg-canvas text-[13px] font-semibold text-ink">
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Content Type / Reading Mode</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Typical Adult Speed (WPM)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-3">Why Your Pace Shifts</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline text-body">
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Light Narrative Fiction</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">260 – 320 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Familiar conversational vocabulary and forward plot momentum</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">General Non-Fiction &amp; News</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">238 WPM (220–260)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Balanced mix of narrative explanations and factual concepts</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Dense Academic / Technical Prose</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">120 – 180 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Frequent pauses to decode formulas, jargon, and logical steps</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Proofreading &amp; Line Editing</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">100 – 150 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Deliberate word-by-word inspection to catch typos and grammar</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Reading Out Loud (Oral Pace)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">150 – 183 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Limited by physical articulation, breathing, and vocal pacing</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink">Audiobook Narration (1.0x)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-ink tabular-nums">150 – 160 WPM</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3">Professional studio narration paced for clear listener comprehension</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Why Reading Out Loud Is Slower Than Silent Reading
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				When you read out loud, your speed is capped by how fast your lips, tongue, and vocal cords can physically form words—averaging <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">183 WPM</strong> for adults, and closer to <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">130 to 150 WPM</strong> when delivering a clear public presentation. Silent reading removes that physical bottleneck, allowing your eyes and brain to process phrases roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">30% to 50% faster</strong> than spoken speech.
			</p>

			<ul class="list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>
					If you are preparing a talk, podcast script, or wedding toast, use our <a href="/speech-time-calculator/" class="font-medium text-vercel-blue underline decoration-vercel-blue/30 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-vercel-blue-deep hover:decoration-vercel-blue">Speech Time Calculator</a> to convert your word count into exact speaking minutes.
				</li>
				<li>
					If you prefer listening to books or comparing narration speeds (1.0x vs. 1.5x), try our <a href="/audiobook-speed-calculator/" class="font-medium text-vercel-blue underline decoration-vercel-blue/30 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-vercel-blue-deep hover:decoration-vercel-blue">Audiobook Speed Calculator</a> to see how quickly you can finish your next title.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Matching Your Speed to Your Purpose
			</h3>

			<p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-body">
				Trying to read a dense philosophy textbook at 350 WPM is just as counterproductive as reading a casual beach novel at 130 WPM. True reading mastery is <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">flexibility</strong>: slowing down when every clause carries heavy meaning, and letting your eyes glide smoothly when the prose is straightforward.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mx-auto mt-12 max-w-[880px] space-y-6 border-t border-hairline px-4 pt-12 pb-16 sm:px-6 md:mt-16 md:pt-16">
			<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">
				Frequently Asked Questions
			</h2>

			<div class="divide-y divide-hairline rounded-2xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-elevated">
				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6" open>
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>How do I test my reading speed accurately?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						To measure your true reading speed, time yourself reading an unfamiliar passage silently at your natural pace without pausing or re-reading lines, then answer comprehension questions from memory. Divide the total word count by your reading time in seconds and multiply by 60 to get your raw WPM, then multiply that score by your quiz accuracy percentage to find your Effective WPM.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>What is a good reading speed score for an adult?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						For most adults reading standard non-fiction, a good reading speed is between 238 and 300 WPM with at least 75% to 80% comprehension. Scores above 320 WPM with strong recall put you ahead of roughly 80% of adult readers, while scores above 400 WPM are considered fast.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>How many words per minute should a 6th grader read?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						A typical 6th grader (ages 11–12) reads roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">150 to 205 words per minute (WPM)</strong> during silent reading with at least 75% comprehension, and about <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">130 to 160 WPM</strong> when reading aloud for oral fluency benchmarks.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Is 200 WPM a slow reading speed?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						No, 200 WPM is not unusually slow—it sits right at the lower end of the normal adult range (200 to 300 WPM). If you are reading dense technical textbooks, academic papers, or material in a second language, 200 WPM is a healthy, careful pace that supports deep understanding.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Is reading 400 WPM fast?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Yes, 400 WPM is nearly 70% faster than the adult average of 238 WPM. At 400 WPM, you can read roughly 24,000 words per hour—or about 80 standard paperback pages per hour—making it a strong, efficient pace for university students, researchers, and avid readers.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Is 500 WPM considered fast reading?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Yes, 500 WPM is very fast and places you in roughly the top 1% to 2% of natural adult readers, provided your comprehension stays above 75%. At 500 WPM, you read 30,000 words—or roughly 100 paperback pages—every hour.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Are 800, 900, or 1,000 WPM reading speeds humanly possible?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Eye-tracking and cognitive psychology research shows that 700 to 900 WPM is the upper biological ceiling for genuine reading with full comprehension. Speeds of 1,000 WPM or higher are classified as skimming—where your eyes sample keywords and skip filler words rather than reading every sentence.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>What is the top 1% of reading speeds?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Among adults who maintain high comprehension (80% or higher on recall tests), the top 1% of readers typically read between 500 and 700 WPM. Claims of 1,500+ WPM almost always reflect rapid skimming rather than full-text comprehension.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>How fast is 100 pages an hour, and is 20 pages an hour slow?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Reading 100 standard paperback pages (~300 words per page) in an hour equals 500 WPM, which is exceptionally fast. Conversely, 20 pages an hour equals roughly 100 WPM—slow for casual fiction, though completely normal when studying complex STEM textbooks, legal documents, or philosophy.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Can you read 3,000 words in 15 minutes?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Yes—reading 3,000 words in 15 minutes requires an average speed of 200 WPM (3,000 divided by 15 equals 200). Because 200 WPM is right at the baseline adult reading speed, almost any adult reader can finish a 3,000-word article in 15 minutes comfortably.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Is reading 200 pages a day a lot?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						Yes, 200 pages a day is a heavy daily reading volume. At 300 words per page (60,000 words total), finishing 200 pages takes an average adult reader (238 WPM) roughly 4 hours and 12 minutes of focused reading per day, or about 2 hours for a fast 500 WPM reader.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>What is the 50-page book rule?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						The 50-page book rule (popularized by librarian Nancy Pearl) is a practical reading habit: give any new book 50 pages to capture your interest, and if it still feels like a chore by page 50, put it down guilt-free and move on to a better book. For readers over age 50, the rule playfully adjusts to subtracting your age from 100 (100 minus age equals pages to try).
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>What is the 5-finger rule for books?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						The 5-finger rule is a quick way for kids, parents, and students to check if a book matches their reading level. Open to any full page and raise one finger for each unfamiliar or difficult word: 0–1 fingers means the book is very easy, 2–3 fingers is the ideal "just right" challenge, and 4–5 fingers means the vocabulary may be too frustrating without help.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Is reading speed related to IQ?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						No—reading speed has only a weak correlation with general IQ. How fast you read is driven much more by instant word recognition, vocabulary size, and daily reading practice than raw intelligence, and many brilliant thinkers intentionally read slowly to analyze complex ideas.
					</p>
				</details>

				<details class="group p-5 sm:p-6">
					<summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 text-[16px] font-semibold text-ink">
						<span>Do people with ADHD read faster or slower?</span>
						<span class="text-muted transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
					</summary>
					<p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-body">
						People with ADHD can experience both depending on the text. Routine or uninteresting passages often result in slower speeds (140 to 190 WPM) due to attentional drift and re-reading lines, while high-interest topics can trigger hyperfocus, pushing reading speeds above 300 WPM.
					</p>
				</details>
			</div>
		</section>
